<article class="env-lemma" id="09K6">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/09K6">Lemma <span data-tag="09K6">22.8.1</span>.</a> Let $(A, \text{d})$ be a differential graded algebra. Let $0 \to K \to L \to M \to 0$ be an admissible short exact sequence of differential graded $A$-modules. The triangle </p>
  <div class="equation" id="09K7">
    <span class="equation-label"><a href="/tag/09K7" data-tag="09K7">22.8.1.1</a></span>
  <div>\begin{equation}  \label{dga-equation-triangle-associated-to-admissible-ses} K \to L \to M \xrightarrow {\delta } K[1] \end{equation}</div>
</div>
  <p> with $\delta $ as in Lemma <a href="/tag/09JU" data-tag="09JU">22.7.2</a> is, up to canonical isomorphism in $K(\text{Mod}_{(A, \text{d})})$, independent of the choices made in Lemma <a href="/tag/09JU" data-tag="09JU">22.7.2</a>. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Namely, let $(s', \pi ')$ be a second choice of splittings as in Lemma <a href="/tag/09JU" data-tag="09JU">22.7.2</a>. Then we claim that $\delta $ and $\delta '$ are homotopic. Namely, write $s' = s + \alpha \circ h$ and $\pi ' = \pi + g \circ \beta $ for some unique homomorphisms of $A$-modules $h : M \to K$ and $g : M \to K$ of degree $-1$. Then $g = -h$ and $g$ is a homotopy between $\delta $ and $\delta '$. The computations are done in the proof of Homology, Lemma <a href="/tag/011L" data-tag="011L">12.14.12</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>